--- Finding: a float written into an item of a list of lists of ints
--- (`g[0][0] = 2.5`) does not widen the lists to floats: the compiled
--- program does not build, and the interpreter keeps 2.5 among the ints.
---
--- Interpreted:
---     [[2.5, 0], [0, 0]]
--- Compiled:
---     error[E0308]: mismatched types
---       --> src/main.rs:42:174
---     error: the compiled program does not build — the Rust compiler refused
---     what Crow accepted, a bug in Crow, not in this program (`crow run -i`
---     runs it meanwhile)
--- Expected (spec, Variables: "a list ... of ints handed a float holds
--- floats from then on", "an item of a list of floats assigned an int holds
--- its float"): both modes build and print [[2.5, 0], [0, 0]], the lists
--- held as floats.
---
--- `crow build --ir` keeps `g: List(List(Int))` and writes `g[0][0] = 2.5`
--- into it; the Rust assigns an f64 into a Vec<i64>. A flat list widens
--- (`xs = for c in range[2]` / `0`, then `xs[0] = 2.5` agrees in both
--- modes); a list of lists built by a nested collecting loop fails the same
--- way as this push-built one. Found writing a 2D Abelian sandpile variant.
row = []
row = push[row, 0]
row = push[row, 0]
g = []
g = push[g, row]
g = push[g, row]
g[0][0] = 2.5
log[g]
